<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_icra_sign">
  <!-- Push a letter into the gap in a sign. Seven fixed letters stand in a
       row spelling "ICRA 2026" with the C's own slot left empty; the goal
       is that slot, so a solved run completes the word.

       IsaacGym conf/task/sim_task05.yaml, which spells "IROS 2026" and
       pushes its S. Same task, different word: the row spacing, glyph
       sizes, goal orientation and start pose are the source's; only the
       letters change.

       This is the one scene whose pushed object is not the T, so it does
       not share tee.xml -- it declares its own table, camera, object and
       goal below. The arm, physics and room still come from common.xml.

       Its xy re-origin also differs: the pushed letter starts at
       (0.7, -0.40) in IsaacGym (sim_task05.yaml's object-side
       `initial_actor_positions`; conf/actors/S.yaml itself leaves init_pos
       commented out because that task is normally launched with a
       `scenario_id` from examples/xarm6_stick_push/scenario_init_poses.yaml
       that overwrites it). So the shift here is (-0.7, +0.40), not the
       T-block scenes' (-0.7, +0.45).

       WHY THE C IS THE PUSHED LETTER. In "IROS" the source pushes the S,
       the only glyph in that word with no enclosed counter. "ICRA" has two
       such glyphs, I and C, and C is the closer analogue of S: an open
       curve with one deep concavity, where I is a plain bar. It also
       matters mechanically -- a footprint is a single `Polygon`, which
       cannot express a hole, so pushing the R or the A would force the
       planner to treat that letter's counter as solid while the simulator
       has a hole there. tests/test_scenes.py could not catch that
       divergence, since it only checks the outline both ways.

       THE OBSTACLE GLYPHS ARE THE SOURCE'S OWN MESHES. Each is its
       `*_centered.ply` (scale 0.001) converted to OBJ under assets/. MJX
       collides a mesh as its convex hull, so `maxhullvert` caps that hull
       and oim/utils/scenes.py carries the matching 2D hull. For letters
       that hull is only 0.84-0.94x the bounding box it replaces -- a
       letter's outline is mostly its bounding box once convexified -- so
       the swap is worth far more visually than geometrically. It is still
       worth making: the row now reads as a sign rather than as slabs.

       WHY THE PUSHED C IS STILL THREE BOXES, NOT A MESH. `alphabets/` has
       no C at all, so there is nothing to load. But even with one, a mesh
       would be *worse* here than boxes: MJX would collide it as its convex
       hull, which for a C is a filled rectangle -- the notch, the single
       feature the whole task turns on, would exist in the render and not
       in the physics. Three boxes are a convex decomposition, so the notch
       survives into contact. The same reasoning is why the T-block scenes
       use boxes: tee_block.urdf is itself two boxes, not a mesh. -->
  <include file="common.xml"/>

  <asset>
    <!-- Paths are relative to *this* file, matching xarm6.xml's convention.
         digit_2 and digit_2b share one mesh: "2026" has two 2s.

         Every mesh here is a `normalize_glyphs.py` output: already metres,
         already in this scene's convention (x = width, y = cap height,
         z = thickness), every glyph at the same 0.103 m cap height and
         0.025 m thickness with its own width.

         `scale="1 1 3"` on the OBSTACLE glyphs, and only in z: at their
         authored 0.025 m they stood exactly as tall as the pushed C, so
         riding over one cost the block no lift at all and runs were
         observed pushing the C straight across a letter. At 0.075 m an
         obstacle is three times the C's own height. The x/y factors stay
         1 deliberately -- a scale there would mean the asset and the row
         disagreed about the typeface, which is what `normalize_glyphs.py`
         exists to prevent, and it would silently invalidate the 2D hulls
         `oim/utils/scenes.py` carries.

         `glyph_c` is NOT scaled: it is the pushed object (the block's
         visual geom, plus the goal and local_goal markers), so scaling it
         would grow the thing being pushed rather than what it must go
         around. Re-run `glyph_hulls.py` after touching any of this;
         MuJoCo re-frames a mesh on load, so even a z-only scale moves the
         plan hull by a few tenths of a millimetre. -->
    <mesh name="glyph_i" file="assets/glyph_i.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
    <mesh name="glyph_a" file="assets/glyph_a.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
    <!-- The pushed C. Visual only -- see the `block` body below. -->
    <mesh name="glyph_c" file="assets/glyph_c.obj"/>
    <mesh name="glyph_r" file="assets/glyph_r.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
    <mesh name="glyph_0" file="assets/glyph_0.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
    <mesh name="glyph_2" file="assets/glyph_2.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
    <mesh name="glyph_6" file="assets/glyph_6.obj" maxhullvert="32" scale="1 1 3"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- The lab table, same geom as tee.xml's (see there). Spans
         x in [-0.05, 0.75], y in [-0.7615, 0.7615], top surface at z = 0.
         The letter row spans y in [-0.565, 0.641] and x in [0.4485, 0.5515],
         so the whole sign stays on it with 0.12 m to spare at either end. -->
    <geom name="table" type="box" size="0.40 0.7615 0.455" pos="0.35 0 -0.455"
      contype="3" conaffinity="3" rgba="0.95 0.95 0.95 1"/>

    <!-- Same framing as the T-block scenes' camera (azimuth=180,
         elevation=-25) but pulled back to distance 2.1 and centred on this
         scene's own workspace, lookat (-0.05, 0.42, 0.15): the letter row
         is 1.25 m long, far wider than that layout, and at 1.7 the "I" and
         the "6" fell outside the frame. -->
    <camera name="front" pos="2.3033 0.0400 1.0375"
      xyaxes="0.0000 1.0000 -0.0000 -0.4226 0.0000 0.9063"/>

    <!-- Mirrors the C's geoms exactly, at the goal pose: the empty slot in
         the row, second from the top, at (0.9, 0.45) in IsaacGym's
         coordinates -> (0.2, 0.85) here. Orientation is the source's own
         letter quat, init_ori [0, 0, 0.7071, -0.7071] (xyzw) = -90 degrees
         about z, which as an MJCF wxyz quat is (0.70711, 0, 0, -0.70711).
         The C spawns unrotated, so this task needs a quarter turn as well
         as a translation. -->
    <body name="goal" mocap="true" pos="0.5 -0.40 0.0125" quat="0.70711 0 0 0.70711">
      <geom class="goal" name="goal_c" type="mesh" mesh="glyph_c"/>
    </body>

    <!-- Ghost of the pushed object at the object block's horizon endpoint
         x^{o*}_H, moved every control step by the runners (mocap, so it is
         written straight into mocap_pos/mocap_quat and never simulated).
         Geoms mirror `block`'s exactly, at the same z, so its overlap with
         the block reads the same way the goal marker's does. `pos`/`quat`
         here are only the pre-first-plan placeholder; oim/runtime/mjcf.py
         parks it on the block's start pose. -->
    <body name="local_goal" mocap="true" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0125" quat="1 0 0 0">
      <geom class="local_goal" name="local_goal_c" type="mesh"
            mesh="glyph_c"/>
    </body>

    <!-- The pushed C. The body name "block" and the joint names T_x/T_y/T_z
         are what oim/tasks/pusht.py looks up, so they are the same in every
         PushT scene regardless of the object's shape. Same frictionloss
         tuning as tee.xml (mu*m*g = 7.848, c*r*mu*m*g = 0.47088) and the
         same 2.0 kg total, split across the three geoms in proportion to
         their area so the mass is distributed like the letter, not lumped.

         0.070 x 0.103 m overall with a 0.020 m stroke. The height and
         thickness are the source glyphs' own (every letter in
         assets/urdf/alphabets measures 100-103 mm tall and 25 mm thick);
         the width sits between the measured O (73.6 mm) and 0 (67.9 mm),
         the two roundest glyphs in that font, since there is no C mesh to
         measure. -->
    <body name="block" pos="0.0 0.0 0.0125">
      <joint name="T_x" type="slide" axis="1 0 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_y" type="slide" axis="0 1 0" damping="0.0"/>
      <joint name="T_z" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <!-- Out-of-plane DoFs, so the block falls when it leaves the table
           instead of hovering. Declared after T_x/T_y/T_z so the SE(2) pose
           stays at the first three qpos entries, which oim/tasks/pusht.py
           reads positionally. No frictionloss: planar resistance stays the
           three calibrated values above -- see the <contact> pairs below. -->
      <joint name="T_zs" type="slide" axis="0 0 1" damping="0.0"/>
      <!-- VISUAL ONLY (contype/conaffinity 0), and mass 0 so the three
           boxes below still carry the whole 2.0 kg -- mu*m*g = 7.848 has
           to keep matching the frictionloss above, so a visual geom must
           not add inertia.

           The collision geometry stays the three boxes. A mesh geom would
           be collided by MJX as its CONVEX HULL, which for a C is a filled
           rectangle: the notch -- the one feature this whole task turns
           on, since the goal is the empty C slot in the row -- would exist
           in the render and not in the physics. Three boxes are a convex
           decomposition, so the notch survives into contact. The boxes are
           drawn transparent rather than deleted; restore an alpha to see
           what is actually being collided.

           0.0966 x 0.103 m with a 0.032 m stroke, matching glyph_c.obj:
           its spine spans x in [-0.0482, -0.0163] at mid-height, which is
           half_width 0.0483 and half_stroke 0.016. Masses are the three
           boxes' raw areas in proportion, as before, which for this
           decomposition is independent of the stroke: 0.696 / 0.652 /
           0.652 summing to the 2.0 kg the friction budget assumes. -->
      <geom name="c_visual" type="mesh" mesh="glyph_c" contype="0"
            conaffinity="0" mass="0" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 1"/>
      <geom name="c_spine" type="box" size="0.0160 0.0515 0.0125" pos="-0.0323 0.0 0.0" mass="0.0348" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 0"/>
      <geom name="c_top_bar" type="box" size="0.0483 0.0160 0.0125" pos="0.0 0.0355 0.0" mass="0.0326" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 0"/>
      <geom name="c_bot_bar" type="box" size="0.0483 0.0160 0.0125" pos="0.0 -0.0355 0.0" mass="0.0326" rgba="0.2 0.45 0.85 0"/>
    </body>

    <!-- "ICRA 2026" minus its C, one static geom per glyph. Every slot
         shares init_pos x = 0.9 (-> 0.2 here) and init_ori
         [0, 0, 0.7071, -0.7071] (xyzw) = -90 degrees about z; `euler` below
         is in degrees, per <compiler angle="degree"> in ../xarm6/xarm6.xml.
         The y values are IsaacGym's own row: 0.6, 0.45, 0.3, 0.15 for the
         four letters, then -0.1, -0.25, -0.4, -0.55 for the year, all
         shifted by +0.40. The gap at y = 0.85 is where the C goes.

         Six of the seven are the source's own `*_centered.ply`, converted
         to OBJ and vendored under assets/. The A is not: `alphabets/` ships
         0, 2, 6, I, O, R and S -- enough for the source's "IROS 2026" but
         not for "ICRA" -- so it is built here from three boxes, which is
         also what the pushed C is and for the same reason (see above). It
         reuses R's bounding box, the same font at the same cap height. -->
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_I" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_i" pos="0.5 -0.55 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_R" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_r" pos="0.5 -0.25 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>

    <!-- The A is now the source's own mesh like every other glyph; it
         used to be three boxes because `alphabets/` shipped no A. -->
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="letter_A" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_a" pos="0.5 -0.1 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>

    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_2" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_2" pos="0.5 0.15 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_0" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_0" pos="0.5 0.3 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_2b" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_2" pos="0.5 0.45 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="digit_6" type="mesh"
      mesh="glyph_6" pos="0.5 0.6 0.0375" euler="0 0 90"/>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- The letter's planar resistance is the table CONTACT's, not a constant
       `frictionloss` bound on its joints: condim="3" with mu = 0.3, the lab
       table's own coefficient and the same mu the analytic limit surface
       assumes, so friction is mu*N and rises when the pusher presses down.
       `frictionloss` is a fixed force bound instead, so pressing on the
       letter would cost nothing and the simulator could not represent the
       one thing that makes top-riding expensive.

       `solimp` stays stiff because a soft normal constraint lets the letter
       sink into the table under a press, silently reducing N.

       mu*m*g = 0.2943 N is the NOMINAL budget that
       `PlanarPushingObject.wrench_limit` and every ADMM normalization read.
       The torque channel is an outcome of the footprint's geometry rather
       than a number declared here, so `limit_surface_radius` in
       oim/utils/scenes.py is measured against this letter. -->
  <contact>
    <pair geom1="c_spine" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
    <pair geom1="c_top_bar" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
    <pair geom1="c_bot_bar" geom2="table" condim="3" friction="0.3 0.3 0.005 0.0001 0.0001" solimp="0.999 0.9999 0.0001"/>
  </contact>

  <sensor>
    <framepos name="position" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
    <framequat name="orientation" objtype="body" objname="block" reftype="body" refname="goal"/>
  </sensor>

  <!-- This scene's own starting pose -- the four T scenes share
       tee_start.xml's instead. Same lab arm home
       (`xarm6_arm_start_deg = [49.2, 34.8, -80.6, 0, 45.9]` deg in
       radians); what differs is the pushed object, a letter that starts at
       (0.3, +0.400) rather than the T's (0.381, +0.400).

       Same y as every other scene's start, by the same rule -- start on the
       +y half, finish on the -y half -- but 0.2 m nearer the arm in x, so
       the C begins clear of the row's own x band ([0.4485, 0.5515]) and has
       to travel the length of the sign and tuck in sideways rather than be
       pushed straight at its slot. The C's vertical DoF is 0: its body sits
       at the glyphs' own 0.0125 m half-thickness, resting on the table. -->
  <keyframe>
    <key name="start"
      qpos="0.0 -0.785 -0.785 0.0 1.5708 0.3 0.400 0.0 0.0"/>
  </keyframe>

  <statistic center="0.40 0.04 0" extent="1.0"/>
</mujoco>
